His work spans across
various high impact areas,

17
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:53,040
ranging from self-driving cars,
robotics, computer control,

18
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:54,840
and Minecraft AI agents.

19
00:00:54,840 --> 00:00:56,370
With that, I'll hand it to him.

20
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:56,870
take.

21
00:00:56,870 --> 00:00:58,370
It away.

22
00:00:58,370 --> 00:01:01,610
Yes, excited to be here.

23
00:01:01,610 --> 00:01:02,750
Great.

24
00:01:02,750 --> 00:01:04,170
So yeah, excited to be here.

25
00:01:04,170 --> 00:01:08,090
And the topic for
this lecture is

26
00:01:08,090 --> 00:01:10,190
we want to talk about
a lot of new things

27
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:12,690
that are happening in
the AI world right now.

28
00:01:12,690 --> 00:01:15,390
So there's been a lot of
developments with agents

29
00:01:15,390 --> 00:01:18,020
And all the new models
that are coming out.

30
00:01:18,020 --> 00:01:21,440
And it seems like you have
some of superintelligence when

31
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:25,490
it comes to chat and
reasoning already there,

32
00:01:25,490 --> 00:01:26,970
compared to average humans.

33
00:01:26,970 --> 00:01:29,000
And it's going to
be very interesting,

34
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,570
over the next few years,
as we figure out what

35
00:01:32,570 --> 00:01:34,004
does intelligence look like?

36
00:01:34,004 --> 00:01:36,530

37
00:01:36,530 --> 00:01:38,420
What is something like AGI?

38
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:39,630
And what's the form factor?

39
00:01:39,630 --> 00:01:41,310
How can this be
something that's useful?

40
00:01:41,310 --> 00:01:43,315
And how will this be
applied in society?

41
00:01:43,315 --> 00:01:46,010

42
00:01:46,010 --> 00:01:47,270
Cool.

43
00:01:47,270 --> 00:01:50,660
So let's take the first
thing we want to touch on.

44
00:01:50,660 --> 00:01:53,090
What does AGI look like?

45
00:01:53,090 --> 00:01:55,250
AGI is such an abstract
concept right now.

46
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:58,500
No one has visualized it
or given it a meaning.

47
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:00,780
Is this some sort
of supercomputer?

48
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:03,600
Is it just like ChatGPT
but just 10x better?

49
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:05,970
Is it something that's more
of a personal companion?

50
00:02:05,970 --> 00:02:08,078
It's something that's
embedded in your life.

51
00:02:08,078 --> 00:02:09,120
And that's not clear yet.

52
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:10,789
And those are the
questions, I think,

53
00:02:10,789 --> 00:02:12,430
we really need to
go and figure out.

54
00:02:12,430 --> 00:02:16,340

55
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:20,280
This is one diagram
on how AI agents work.

56
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:22,850
So this is an architecture from
a OpenAI researcher Lillian

57
00:02:22,850 --> 00:02:23,460
Wang.

58
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:26,840
She recently left and
joined a new company.

59
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:30,560
So this is showing how
you can think about agents

60
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:34,350
and how they can be broken
down into different subparts.

61
00:02:34,350 --> 00:02:36,350
And there's a lot
of different things

62
00:02:36,350 --> 00:02:38,820
that you require to
make this agents work.

63
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:40,560
So the first layer is memory.

64
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:42,920
You need to have some
short-term memory.

65
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:44,670
You want to have some
of long term memory.

66
00:02:44,670 --> 00:02:47,330
And this is, like, you have
some short representation that's

67
00:02:47,330 --> 00:02:49,790
maybe a chat window if
you're using ChatGPT.

68
00:02:49,790 --> 00:02:52,430
And you might also have a
personal history of the user

69
00:02:52,430 --> 00:02:54,780
where, OK, this is maybe
what the user likes.

70
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:56,463
This is what they don't like.

71
00:02:56,463 --> 00:02:58,130
The second thing that
you need is tools.

72
00:02:58,130 --> 00:03:02,470
You want this kind of agents
to be able to use tools

73
00:03:02,470 --> 00:03:03,920
like how humans use tools.

74
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:06,320
So you want them to be
able to use calculators.

75
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:10,240
You want them to be able to use
calendars, web search, coding,

76
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:11,590
and so on.

77
00:03:11,590 --> 00:03:14,360
The third part over here is you
want to have advanced planning.

78
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:16,570
And that means you
want to-- the agents

79
00:03:16,570 --> 00:03:19,100
to be able to use reflection,
where if something goes wrong,

80
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:21,550
they can have
failover mechanisms,

81
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:23,150
error correct, and recover.

82
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:24,790
You want self-criticism.

83
00:03:24,790 --> 00:03:27,130
And you want a
decomposition, where

84
00:03:27,130 --> 00:03:28,490
you have chains of thoughts.

85
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:30,950
So the agent can do their
own reasoning loops.

86
00:03:30,950 --> 00:03:34,210
They can also break down a
complex task into subgoals.

87
00:03:34,210 --> 00:03:35,860
And the final
fourth ingredient is

88
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:37,360
actions, where you
want these agents

89
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:40,610
to be able to act on your
behalf and go do things.

90
00:03:40,610 --> 00:03:43,300
And this is high
level encapsulates

91
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:46,310
how agents look
like, fundamentally.

92
00:03:46,310 --> 00:03:48,220
And this is maybe what will--

93
00:03:48,220 --> 00:03:51,187
as these systems become
more powerful over time,

94
00:03:51,187 --> 00:03:53,270
will eventually lead to
something that's like AGI.

95
00:03:53,270 --> 00:03:56,210

96
00:03:56,210 --> 00:04:00,890
This is one thing that
we're also building.

97
00:04:00,890 --> 00:04:05,120
So I recently started this new
AI lab called AGI Incorporated.

98
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:08,090
And we're looking a lot into,
like, what does AGI look

99
00:04:08,090 --> 00:04:09,320
like for everyday purposes?

100
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:11,155
And how can this be
applied to daily life?

101
00:04:11,155 --> 00:04:13,850

102
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:17,089
This is one of the demos
of some technologies

103
00:04:17,089 --> 00:04:18,529
we built in the past.

104
00:04:18,529 --> 00:04:21,980
This shows how an AI agent can
be applied in the real world.

105
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:24,410
So this is a bit old.

106
00:04:24,410 --> 00:04:27,320
And it shows how
an AI agent can be

107
00:04:27,320 --> 00:04:29,850
applied to pass a real
driving test in California.

108
00:04:29,850 --> 00:04:36,238
And so this is an actual DMV
test that the agent took.

109
00:04:36,238 --> 00:04:38,280
Let me share the screen
and talk about the setup.

110
00:04:38,280 --> 00:04:43,310
So in this screen,
what's happening is

111
00:04:43,310 --> 00:04:47,240
there's someone attempting
the DMV online test.

112
00:04:47,240 --> 00:04:49,652
There's a human who has their
hands over the keyboard.

113
00:04:49,652 --> 00:04:51,360
They're not actually
touching the screen.

114
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:53,693
And it's an agent that's going
and taking all the exams.

115
00:04:53,693 --> 00:04:56,090
And there's 40
questions in this test.

116
00:04:56,090 --> 00:04:57,757
And the agent is really good.

117
00:04:57,757 --> 00:04:59,340
So it can go and
pass the whole thing.

118
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:01,080
And we did this live.

119
00:05:01,080 --> 00:05:05,370
So the DMV was actually screen
recording what we're doing.

120
00:05:05,370 --> 00:05:08,210
They were also watching
the person on camera.

121
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:11,330
But even then, the
agent was successfully

122
00:05:11,330 --> 00:05:15,110
able to evade the whole
setup and pass the exam.

123
00:05:15,110 --> 00:05:16,248
So this was really fun.

124
00:05:16,248 --> 00:05:17,790
We did this as a
[INAUDIBLE] attempt,

125
00:05:17,790 --> 00:05:20,780
so we informed the DMV
afterwards that we did this.

126
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:23,390
Funnily enough, they actually
sent us a driving license,

127
00:05:23,390 --> 00:05:24,570
afterwards.

128
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:26,330
So that was really
fun, actually.

129
00:05:26,330 --> 00:05:29,630
So at the end, the agent is
able to pass and get a pass,

130
00:05:29,630 --> 00:05:33,410
get a full score
on this test here.

131
00:05:33,410 --> 00:05:35,513
And so yeah, so this is
a very fun experiment

132
00:05:35,513 --> 00:05:37,680
showing how agents can be
applied in the real world.

133
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:40,700
And there's so many
things that are possible.

134
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:43,680
In this vein, how can we
make agents more useful,

135
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:45,410
apply them in real life.

136
00:05:45,410 --> 00:05:47,940
We have been working on a
lot of different efforts,

137
00:05:47,940 --> 00:05:51,670
along with a lot of
the AI community.

138
00:05:51,670 --> 00:05:54,380
One of those things
is agent evaluations.

139
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:56,980
How can we evaluate this kind
of agents in the real world

140
00:05:56,980 --> 00:06:00,040
and make sure we have
standards and benchmarks that

141
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:02,770
allows us to know, how well
are these agents working

142
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:05,660
on different websites
or different use cases?

143
00:06:05,660 --> 00:06:06,590
How can we trust them?

144
00:06:06,590 --> 00:06:10,660
How can know where to deploy
them and how to use them?

145
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:14,060
Another thing we are doing have
been doing is agent training.

146
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:16,720
Can we train agents
to be able to do

147
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:18,580
advanced planning,
self-correction,

148
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:19,910
and improve themselves?

149
00:06:19,910 --> 00:06:22,750
And this uses a combination
of reinforcement learning

150
00:06:22,750 --> 00:06:24,770
and a bunch of other
advanced techniques.

151
00:06:24,770 --> 00:06:27,190
And finally, we have
also been looking a lot

152
00:06:27,190 --> 00:06:28,670
into agent communication.

153
00:06:28,670 --> 00:06:31,715
How can you have an agent
communicate with other agents?

154
00:06:31,715 --> 00:06:34,090
And there's been a lot of new
breakthroughs in this area,

155
00:06:34,090 --> 00:06:34,850
recently.

156
00:06:34,850 --> 00:06:37,090
So if you've looked at
model context protocol MCP,

157
00:06:37,090 --> 00:06:39,910
that's a very new thing
that has been coming out.

158
00:06:39,910 --> 00:06:42,448
Similarly, there's a
lot of work around A2A,

159
00:06:42,448 --> 00:06:44,740
that's Google's Agent2Agent
communication protocol that

160
00:06:44,740 --> 00:06:46,030
recently came out.

161
00:06:46,030 --> 00:06:50,790
We also have been working on
some open source projects called

162
00:06:50,790 --> 00:06:54,180
Agent protocol, where
we have been allowing

163
00:06:54,180 --> 00:06:56,680
other different kind of agents
to communicate to each other.

164
00:06:56,680 --> 00:06:58,138
So you can have a
coding agent that

165
00:06:58,138 --> 00:07:00,760
can talk to a web agent that
can talk to an API based agent

166
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:01,330
and so on.

167
00:07:01,330 --> 00:07:03,900
And that allows you to do
much, much more complex things

168
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:06,065
than what's possible
with just a single agent.

169
00:07:06,065 --> 00:07:09,150

170
00:07:09,150 --> 00:07:10,320
Cool.

171
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:13,920
So before we dive more deeper
into how a lot of these things

172
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:18,130
works, I want to bring out,
like, why do we need agents?

173
00:07:18,130 --> 00:07:19,195
Why are they useful?

174
00:07:19,195 --> 00:07:21,070
Why do we actually want
to go and build them?

175
00:07:21,070 --> 00:07:25,350
And there's a lot of things
we need to think about here.

176
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:28,260
And I will touch on a
lot of different topics

177
00:07:28,260 --> 00:07:30,690
in the introduction, going
from the architectures,

178
00:07:30,690 --> 00:07:33,820
building more human-like agents,
using computer interactions,

179
00:07:33,820 --> 00:07:37,240
maybe memory communication, and
what are the future directions.

180
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:40,350

181
00:07:40,350 --> 00:07:43,540
So when you think
about building agents,

182
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:46,390
there's a lot of things,
questions you have to answer.

183
00:07:46,390 --> 00:07:48,850
The first one is,
why is this useful?

184
00:07:48,850 --> 00:07:50,290
How can you actually build them?

185
00:07:50,290 --> 00:07:52,005
What are the different
building blocks?

186
00:07:52,005 --> 00:07:53,630
And finally, what
can you do with them?

187
00:07:53,630 --> 00:07:57,300

188
00:07:57,300 --> 00:08:00,060
And to first answer
the why question,

189
00:08:00,060 --> 00:08:03,780
we have this key
thesis that agents

190
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:06,660
will be more efficient
in interfacing

191
00:08:06,660 --> 00:08:09,580
with computers in the digital
world compared to humans.

192
00:08:09,580 --> 00:08:13,320
And that's the reason that we
want to go and apply agents

193
00:08:13,320 --> 00:08:14,950
to be able to do things for us.

194
00:08:14,950 --> 00:08:17,670
So you can imagine you have
an army of virtual assistants

195
00:08:17,670 --> 00:08:20,850
that are fully digital,
that can go and do

196
00:08:20,850 --> 00:08:22,600
whatever you want
on your behalf.

197
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:25,060
And you can just talk to
them using a human interface.

198
00:08:25,060 --> 00:08:27,230
And that's the vision we
have been moving towards.

199
00:08:27,230 --> 00:08:30,510

200
00:08:30,510 --> 00:08:32,970
I also have a blog post about
this called Software 3.0

201
00:08:32,970 --> 00:08:35,190
that you can check
out, which touches

202
00:08:35,190 --> 00:08:36,574
upon some of those ideas.

203
00:08:36,574 --> 00:08:39,390

204
00:08:39,390 --> 00:08:41,340
Cool.

205
00:08:41,340 --> 00:08:43,440
So we want to go
and build agents

206
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:46,810
because, usually, large language
models are not good enough.

207
00:08:46,810 --> 00:08:48,720
And we want action
capabilities that

208
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:51,990
allows us to unlock more
productivity and go do things.

209
00:08:51,990 --> 00:08:54,370
And this also allows us to
build more complex systems.

210
00:08:54,370 --> 00:08:56,745
There's a lot of techniques
involved in actually building

211
00:08:56,745 --> 00:09:00,000
this, such as chaining
different models together,

212
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:04,710
reflection, and a bunch
of other mechanisms.

213
00:09:04,710 --> 00:09:10,420
And as I showed before in the
architecture, on slide two,

214
00:09:10,420 --> 00:09:13,320
there's a lot of different
components-- memory,

215
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:19,860
actions, personalization, access
to the internet, and so on.

216
00:09:19,860 --> 00:09:21,330
And finally, the
question becomes,

217
00:09:21,330 --> 00:09:22,480
what are the
different applications

218
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:23,355
we can apply them to?

219
00:09:23,355 --> 00:09:25,920

220
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:27,420
There's also a
question of, why do

221
00:09:27,420 --> 00:09:29,080
we want to build
human-like agents?

222
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:30,970
Why can't we just
have API agents?

223
00:09:30,970 --> 00:09:34,570
Or why can't we have a bunch
of other kind of agents

224
00:09:34,570 --> 00:09:38,370
you can imagine, which are not
mimicking human interactions?

225
00:09:38,370 --> 00:09:42,335
And one reason we want to push
towards more human-like agents

226
00:09:42,335 --> 00:09:45,600
is these agents can operate
interfaces like how we do.

227
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:47,957
And usually, the
internet, and the web,

228
00:09:47,957 --> 00:09:49,540
and computers are
designed for humans.

229
00:09:49,540 --> 00:09:53,340
So they're designed for
keyboard and mouse interactions,

230
00:09:53,340 --> 00:09:55,450
so that we can go and
navigate interfaces.

231
00:09:55,450 --> 00:09:56,650
We can use our keyboards.

232
00:09:56,650 --> 00:10:00,400
And if agents are able to
use interfaces like we do,

233
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:02,490
that allows them to
directly communicate and do

234
00:10:02,490 --> 00:10:05,520
a lot of things without
changing how current software

235
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:06,460
programs work.

236
00:10:06,460 --> 00:10:08,010
And that becomes
very, very effective

237
00:10:08,010 --> 00:10:11,460
because that allows you to
work on 100% of the internet

238
00:10:11,460 --> 00:10:13,180
without any bottlenecks.

239
00:10:13,180 --> 00:10:15,960
If you think about
APIs, there's only 5%

240
00:10:15,960 --> 00:10:18,805
of APIs on the internet are
public that are accessible.

241
00:10:18,805 --> 00:10:20,430
And it's very hard
to build agents that

242
00:10:20,430 --> 00:10:22,000
are fully reliable over APIs.

243
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:23,430
And so there's a
lot of contention

244
00:10:23,430 --> 00:10:25,452
between human-like
agents versus API agents.

245
00:10:25,452 --> 00:10:27,785
And that's an ongoing battle
that's happening right now.

246
00:10:27,785 --> 00:10:31,110

247
00:10:31,110 --> 00:10:34,380
Second thing is you can imagine
a lot of human-like agents

248
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:36,930
as becoming a digital
extension of you.

249
00:10:36,930 --> 00:10:38,110
So they can learn about you.

250
00:10:38,110 --> 00:10:39,527
They can have a
context about you.

251
00:10:39,527 --> 00:10:43,580
They can do tasks like
how you will do it.

252
00:10:43,580 --> 00:10:46,850
They also have less
restrictive boundaries.

253
00:10:46,850 --> 00:10:49,140
This kind of human-like
agents can handle logins,

254
00:10:49,140 --> 00:10:50,310
they can handle payments.

255
00:10:50,310 --> 00:10:52,640
And they're able to interact
with any of the services

256
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:55,890
without restrictions
in terms of API access.

257
00:10:55,890 --> 00:10:58,130
So you don't need to
pay for using an API

258
00:10:58,130 --> 00:11:00,882
or you don't need to go
to a service provider

259
00:11:00,882 --> 00:11:03,090
and ask them for, can you
give me access to this API?

260
00:11:03,090 --> 00:11:08,300
You can just go and use an
interface like you normally do.

261
00:11:08,300 --> 00:11:12,012
And the final thing is there's
a very simple action space.

262
00:11:12,012 --> 00:11:14,220
This agents only need to
learn how to click and type.

263
00:11:14,220 --> 00:11:16,220
And if they're able to
do that very effectively,

264
00:11:16,220 --> 00:11:19,790
they can generalize
to any interface.

265
00:11:19,790 --> 00:11:21,630
And they can also
improve over time.

266
00:11:21,630 --> 00:11:25,410
So the more you teach them, the
more data you can give them.

267
00:11:25,410 --> 00:11:27,815
They can learn from user
recordings, feedback,

268
00:11:27,815 --> 00:11:29,440
and become better
and better over time.

269
00:11:29,440 --> 00:11:33,260

270
00:11:33,260 --> 00:11:37,940
And so when it comes to this
API versus more direct computer

271
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:42,830
control agents, these are how we
think about the pros and cons.

272
00:11:42,830 --> 00:11:46,130
API agents are usually
easier to build.

273
00:11:46,130 --> 00:11:47,490
They are more controllable.

274
00:11:47,490 --> 00:11:49,370
They are more safer.

275
00:11:49,370 --> 00:11:52,140
But APIs are more
have high viability.

276
00:11:52,140 --> 00:11:55,050
So you have to build
different agents for each API.

277
00:11:55,050 --> 00:11:56,910
And then APIs can keep changing.

278
00:11:56,910 --> 00:11:59,990
You never have a full guarantee
that this agent will always

279
00:11:59,990 --> 00:12:01,670
work 100%.

280
00:12:01,670 --> 00:12:03,750
When it comes to this
more direct interaction

281
00:12:03,750 --> 00:12:07,100
computer controlled agents,
it's easier to take actions,

282
00:12:07,100 --> 00:12:09,080
in this case.

283
00:12:09,080 --> 00:12:12,230
It's also more
freeform interactions

284
00:12:12,230 --> 00:12:14,640
because you're not restricted
by the API boundaries.

285
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:16,713
But it's also hard
to provide guarantees

286
00:12:16,713 --> 00:12:18,630
because you don't know
what the agent will do.

287
00:12:18,630 --> 00:12:22,280
So if anyone here has played
with agents like Operator,

288
00:12:22,280 --> 00:12:24,780
it's a work in progress.

289
00:12:24,780 --> 00:12:26,240
It's not really there.

290
00:12:26,240 --> 00:12:28,290
There's a lot of issues
that it turns into.

291
00:12:28,290 --> 00:12:31,165
And that's the boundaries
where agents are right now.

292
00:12:31,165 --> 00:12:34,550

293
00:12:34,550 --> 00:12:36,540
There's also different
levels of autonomy

294
00:12:36,540 --> 00:12:38,120
when you think about agents.

295
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:40,560
This is usually goes
from level 1 to level 5.

296
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:44,240
So level 1 to level 2 is
when a human is in control

297
00:12:44,240 --> 00:12:46,107
and the agent is
acting like a co-pilot.

298
00:12:46,107 --> 00:12:47,190
So it's helping the human.

299
00:12:47,190 --> 00:12:50,060
And so this is something
like if you've used

300
00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:51,900
like a code editor like Cursor.

301
00:12:51,900 --> 00:12:55,638
That's a L2 agent, where you
have partial automation, where

302
00:12:55,638 --> 00:12:57,930
the human is in control, the
human is writing the code,

303
00:12:57,930 --> 00:12:59,390
but the agent is helping them.

304
00:12:59,390 --> 00:13:01,230
When it comes to
something like L3,

305
00:13:01,230 --> 00:13:05,460
this is where there's still
a human fallback mechanism,

306
00:13:05,460 --> 00:13:06,690
but the agent is in control.

307
00:13:06,690 --> 00:13:09,850
So this is if you use Cursor
Composer, or Windsurf, or any

308
00:13:09,850 --> 00:13:11,850
of the newer code editors
that are more agentic.

309
00:13:11,850 --> 00:13:13,140
The agent is writing
most of the code,

310
00:13:13,140 --> 00:13:15,060
but a human is monitoring,
giving it feedback.

311
00:13:15,060 --> 00:13:15,750
OK, this went wrong.

312
00:13:15,750 --> 00:13:16,830
Can you correct that for me?

313
00:13:16,830 --> 00:13:17,850
Can you fix this issue?

314
00:13:17,850 --> 00:13:20,265
And that is more of a L3 system.

315
00:13:20,265 --> 00:13:21,890
And then you have
more advanced systems

316
00:13:21,890 --> 00:13:23,480
which are like L4 and L5.

317
00:13:23,480 --> 00:13:26,483
And L4 systems, you don't
have a human in the loop.

318
00:13:26,483 --> 00:13:28,650
So it's the agent that's
going and doing everything.

319
00:13:28,650 --> 00:13:31,320
You might still have some of
automated fallback layers.

320
00:13:31,320 --> 00:13:33,872
So if you look at Waymo
in SF, that's an L4 system

321
00:13:33,872 --> 00:13:35,830
because the self-driving
car is driving itself,

322
00:13:35,830 --> 00:13:37,330
but there's human
operators that are

323
00:13:37,330 --> 00:13:40,330
remotely monitoring it, making
sure nothing goes wrong.

324
00:13:40,330 --> 00:13:42,130
And then you have L5 system.

325
00:13:42,130 --> 00:13:43,990
In that case, there's
no humans in the loop.

326
00:13:43,990 --> 00:13:45,130
There's no monitoring.

327
00:13:45,130 --> 00:13:48,360
And the AI agent is able to
operate itself autonomously,

328
00:13:48,360 --> 00:13:50,080
fully, fully, independently.

329
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:56,886

330
00:13:56,886 --> 00:14:01,562
So when we are building this
agents, one hard thing is trust.

331
00:14:01,562 --> 00:14:03,270
How do we trust these
agents are actually

332
00:14:03,270 --> 00:14:05,230
going to go do what
we want them to do?

333
00:14:05,230 --> 00:14:07,980
How can we go and deploy
them in the real world?

334
00:14:07,980 --> 00:14:10,470
To solve these
issues, one effort

335
00:14:10,470 --> 00:14:15,690
that we have been building
is a miniature version

336
00:14:15,690 --> 00:14:17,880
of the internet, where
we have cloned the top 20

337
00:14:17,880 --> 00:14:19,660
websites on the internet.

338
00:14:19,660 --> 00:14:23,370
And we are benchmarking,
how do agents go and perform

339
00:14:23,370 --> 00:14:26,857
on all these interfaces?

340
00:14:26,857 --> 00:14:27,940
And this is actually live.

341
00:14:27,940 --> 00:14:30,240
So you can go check
it out, realevals.xyz.

342
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:32,500
And what we have
done is we have built

343
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:34,900
digital clones of
websites like Airbnb,

344
00:14:34,900 --> 00:14:37,280
and Amazon, and Door
Dash, and LinkedIn.

345
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:40,060
And the agents can go and
navigate these interfaces

346
00:14:40,060 --> 00:14:41,380
on predefined tasks.

347
00:14:41,380 --> 00:14:42,630
And you can get a final score.

348
00:14:42,630 --> 00:14:47,290

349
00:14:47,290 --> 00:14:51,940
This is showing the
evolution results for GPT 4o.

350
00:14:51,940 --> 00:14:55,210
We find that GPT 4o, actually,
is not very good on when it

351
00:14:55,210 --> 00:14:56,540
comes to being agentic.

352
00:14:56,540 --> 00:15:00,520
And it only reaches 14%
successful accuracy,

353
00:15:00,520 --> 00:15:02,320
in this case.

354
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:04,930
We tried this on 11
different environments

355
00:15:04,930 --> 00:15:06,495
that we are showing
on the right.

356
00:15:06,495 --> 00:15:09,730

357
00:15:09,730 --> 00:15:13,940
And we have our
different environments.

358
00:15:13,940 --> 00:15:16,690
We have DashDish, which is our
Door Dash clone, and Omnizon,

359
00:15:16,690 --> 00:15:17,470
and so on.

360
00:15:17,470 --> 00:15:19,220
So you can actually
go and check this out.

361
00:15:19,220 --> 00:15:24,370

362
00:15:24,370 --> 00:15:30,190
We also compare a lot of open
source frameworks out there.

363
00:15:30,190 --> 00:15:32,440
Some of them are like
the OpenAI computer use

364
00:15:32,440 --> 00:15:34,480
model that's powering Operator.

365
00:15:34,480 --> 00:15:36,160
We actually find
it's not very good

366
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:37,640
when it comes to these tasks.

367
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:40,570
So it's only able to
reach maximum 20% accuracy

368
00:15:40,570 --> 00:15:43,750
on some of the environments,
like our email environment

369
00:15:43,750 --> 00:15:45,110
or our calendar environments.

370
00:15:45,110 --> 00:15:47,180
But on a lot of the
other environments,

371
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:49,960
it's not able to actually
go and do really well.

372
00:15:49,960 --> 00:15:52,490
We also tried a bunch of
other frameworks out there--

373
00:15:52,490 --> 00:15:54,657
Stagehand, if you're
familiar-- if you've seen that,

374
00:15:54,657 --> 00:15:56,980
it's an open source
framework for automating

375
00:15:56,980 --> 00:16:01,420
web agents, browser-use, and one
of our own custom agents, which

376
00:16:01,420 --> 00:16:02,630
we're calling Agent-0.

377
00:16:02,630 --> 00:16:04,480
And we find that
agents are still

378
00:16:04,480 --> 00:16:06,677
early when it comes to,
actually, automating

379
00:16:06,677 --> 00:16:07,760
a lot of these interfaces.

380
00:16:07,760 --> 00:16:11,017
And we are able to reach, maybe
I would say, up to 50% success

381
00:16:11,017 --> 00:16:12,850
rate, but a lot of these
agents are actually

382
00:16:12,850 --> 00:16:15,340
failing when you are applying
them in all of these real world

383
00:16:15,340 --> 00:16:15,840
websites.

384
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:20,230

385
00:16:20,230 --> 00:16:22,990
Similarly, we benchmark
all the different models

386
00:16:22,990 --> 00:16:25,870
that are available, including
all the closed source APIs

387
00:16:25,870 --> 00:16:27,790
and all the open source models.

388
00:16:27,790 --> 00:16:31,910
And we find, again,
on ethnic tasks,

389
00:16:31,910 --> 00:16:35,030
most models are
doing decently well,

390
00:16:35,030 --> 00:16:37,160
but no one is really,
really good right now.

391
00:16:37,160 --> 00:16:40,790
The maximum success we have
seen is with Claude 3.7,

392
00:16:40,790 --> 00:16:44,560
where it can reach
around 40% accuracy.

393
00:16:44,560 --> 00:16:49,610
Gemini 2.5 and O3 follow
very closely with it.

394
00:16:49,610 --> 00:16:52,750
The other models
tend to taper off.

395
00:16:52,750 --> 00:16:55,150
And so the interesting
learning has been,

396
00:16:55,150 --> 00:16:57,100
for us, is that a
lot of these models

397
00:16:57,100 --> 00:16:59,690
are not fully ready to be
deployed in the real world

398
00:16:59,690 --> 00:17:02,980
because if you have, say, an
agent that's powered by Claude

399
00:17:02,980 --> 00:17:05,050
and then you're applying
that, you can only

400
00:17:05,050 --> 00:17:07,450
expect a 41% success rate
that this will actually

401
00:17:07,450 --> 00:17:09,069
go do what you want it to do.

402
00:17:09,069 --> 00:17:10,750
And that's not good enough.

403
00:17:10,750 --> 00:17:14,109
And this brings a
question of, what

404
00:17:14,109 --> 00:17:17,060
is it that is required to
make these agents even better?

405
00:17:17,060 --> 00:17:18,190
How can they improve?

406
00:17:18,190 --> 00:17:21,449
And how can they be applied for
your actual practical use cases?

407
00:17:21,449 --> 00:17:24,460

408
00:17:24,460 --> 00:17:27,140
And so this brings
us to our next topic

409
00:17:27,140 --> 00:17:32,700
for the lecture, which is, how
can we train AI agent models?

410
00:17:32,700 --> 00:17:35,690
So how can we have models that
are more custom fine tuned

411
00:17:35,690 --> 00:17:37,480
and are better on
the generic tasks?

412
00:17:37,480 --> 00:17:41,330

413
00:17:41,330 --> 00:17:44,930
This is one of our past works
called Agent Q, which is

414
00:17:44,930 --> 00:17:46,180
a self-improving agent system.

415
00:17:46,180 --> 00:17:54,544

416
00:17:54,544 --> 00:17:56,540
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

417
00:17:56,540 --> 00:18:10,013

418
00:18:10,013 --> 00:18:11,510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

419
00:18:11,510 --> 00:19:02,408

420
00:19:02,408 --> 00:19:04,440
[END PLAYBACK]

421
00:19:04,440 --> 00:19:07,690
So this is Agent Q. That's a
system that can self-improve.

422
00:19:07,690 --> 00:19:11,410
It can learn by
corrections and planning.

423
00:19:11,410 --> 00:19:14,340
And how the system works is
it's able to go and self-correct

424
00:19:14,340 --> 00:19:15,010
itself.

425
00:19:15,010 --> 00:19:16,800
So whenever it
makes a mistake, it

426
00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:18,790
can save that mistake
in its past memory.

427
00:19:18,790 --> 00:19:22,800
And it's able to use that to
do a lot of trial and error

428
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:24,250
learning, similar to humans.

429
00:19:24,250 --> 00:19:27,955
So suppose the first time
you learn how to ride a bike,

430
00:19:27,955 --> 00:19:29,080
you make a lot of mistakes.

431
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:30,160
You fall over a lot of times.

432
00:19:30,160 --> 00:19:32,202
But over time, you're able
to improve your policy

433
00:19:32,202 --> 00:19:34,230
and go and do that really well.

434
00:19:34,230 --> 00:19:36,750
We apply a similar mechanisms
to make these agents actually

435
00:19:36,750 --> 00:19:39,070
work really, really
well in the real world.

436
00:19:39,070 --> 00:19:41,640
And so what's happening
in the system is

437
00:19:41,640 --> 00:19:46,770
the agent can explore
the space of interfaces

438
00:19:46,770 --> 00:19:49,870
and see, what are the things
that it did that went wrong?

439
00:19:49,870 --> 00:19:51,370
What are the things
that went right?

440
00:19:51,370 --> 00:19:52,787
And it's able to
use reinforcement

441
00:19:52,787 --> 00:19:55,950
learning to self-improve and
become better and better.

442
00:19:55,950 --> 00:19:58,450
So Agent Q combines a S
of different techniques.

443
00:19:58,450 --> 00:20:00,840
The first method is
Monte Carlo Tree Search.

444
00:20:00,840 --> 00:20:04,170
This is borrowed from
other RL techniques

445
00:20:04,170 --> 00:20:06,540
like AlphaGo, that
allow you to plan over

446
00:20:06,540 --> 00:20:13,200
a search space of tasks and
unlock advanced reasoning.

447
00:20:13,200 --> 00:20:15,910
A second thing that we do
is self critic mechanisms.

448
00:20:15,910 --> 00:20:18,720
So the agent can self
verify and get feedback

449
00:20:18,720 --> 00:20:19,950
whenever it makes a mistake.

450
00:20:19,950 --> 00:20:23,610
And it's able to learn
from that feedback.

451
00:20:23,610 --> 00:20:28,140
And finally, we use RLAIF
techniques like DPO,

452
00:20:28,140 --> 00:20:30,360
direct preference
optimization to be

453
00:20:30,360 --> 00:20:34,860
able to improve
the agent using RL.

454
00:20:34,860 --> 00:20:37,270
And by combining all these
three techniques together,

455
00:20:37,270 --> 00:20:39,270
we are able to build some
very powerful systems.

456
00:20:39,270 --> 00:20:42,240

457
00:20:42,240 --> 00:20:45,635
Agent Q is also available on
arXiv as a research paper,

458
00:20:45,635 --> 00:20:46,760
so you go and check it out.

459
00:20:46,760 --> 00:20:54,476

460
00:20:54,476 --> 00:20:58,650
For the sake of time, I will
skip some of the lectures here.

461
00:20:58,650 --> 00:21:01,950
But how Agent Q
normally works is

462
00:21:01,950 --> 00:21:06,300
we have this Monte Carlo Tree
Search, where the agent is

463
00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:08,370
exploring the different states.

464
00:21:08,370 --> 00:21:10,800
It's estimating rewards.

465
00:21:10,800 --> 00:21:13,800
If we were to visit
this state, what's

466
00:21:13,800 --> 00:21:18,480
the expected value of the
future predicted reward?

467
00:21:18,480 --> 00:21:22,240
And based on that, it's able to
improve its prediction model.

468
00:21:22,240 --> 00:21:25,535
Should we go take this path or
a different path in the tree?

469
00:21:25,535 --> 00:21:30,450

470
00:21:30,450 --> 00:21:33,420
And then over time, the
agent can become very good

471
00:21:33,420 --> 00:21:35,880
at exploring the right
states and figuring out,

472
00:21:35,880 --> 00:21:37,920
what are the right paths
in the state space?

473
00:21:37,920 --> 00:21:39,087
And what are the wrong ones?

474
00:21:39,087 --> 00:21:43,800

475
00:21:43,800 --> 00:21:46,710
We also do
self-critic mechanism.

476
00:21:46,710 --> 00:21:51,990
In this case, what happens is
if you have a particular task,

477
00:21:51,990 --> 00:21:53,800
in this case, where,
say, a user says,

478
00:21:53,800 --> 00:21:56,770
book me a reservation
for a restaurant,

479
00:21:56,770 --> 00:22:01,560
Fogo de Chao on OpenTable for
two people, on August 14, 2024,

480
00:22:01,560 --> 00:22:02,777
at 7:00 PM.

481
00:22:02,777 --> 00:22:04,860
And this is the current
state of the screen, where

482
00:22:04,860 --> 00:22:06,150
you can see the screenshot.

483
00:22:06,150 --> 00:22:09,300
Then the agent
can go and propose

484
00:22:09,300 --> 00:22:10,570
a bunch of different actions.

485
00:22:10,570 --> 00:22:12,910
So it can choose to go and
select the date and time.

486
00:22:12,910 --> 00:22:15,210
It can choose to also
select the number of people

487
00:22:15,210 --> 00:22:17,550
and then open the date selector.

488
00:22:17,550 --> 00:22:21,990
It can instead search for
Terra Eataly Silicon Valley

489
00:22:21,990 --> 00:22:25,050
restaurant and type that
in the search bar or it

490
00:22:25,050 --> 00:22:30,510
can decide to go to
the OpenTable homepage.

491
00:22:30,510 --> 00:22:33,540
And how the self-critic
mechanism works

492
00:22:33,540 --> 00:22:37,950
is all these proposed actions
are passed to a critic network.

493
00:22:37,950 --> 00:22:42,820
And the critic LLM is able
to go and predict, OK,

494
00:22:42,820 --> 00:22:44,140
what's the best action to take?

495
00:22:44,140 --> 00:22:46,030
And it's able to
give a ranking order.

496
00:22:46,030 --> 00:22:49,410
OK, this is the best action
that we should go and use.

497
00:22:49,410 --> 00:22:50,530
So this is rank 1.

498
00:22:50,530 --> 00:22:51,330
This is rank 2.

499
00:22:51,330 --> 00:22:52,313
And this is ranked 3.

500
00:22:52,313 --> 00:22:54,480
And based on that, we can
go and optimize the system

501
00:22:54,480 --> 00:22:56,885
to take the correct actions
and improve over time.

502
00:22:56,885 --> 00:23:00,960

503
00:23:00,960 --> 00:23:02,970
And finally, we
use reinforcement

504
00:23:02,970 --> 00:23:05,520
learning from human
feedback, where

505
00:23:05,520 --> 00:23:08,200
we used methods
like GRPO and DPO,

506
00:23:08,200 --> 00:23:09,720
which are different
RL algorithms,

507
00:23:09,720 --> 00:23:13,740
to be able to use
all the failures

508
00:23:13,740 --> 00:23:17,090
and successful trajectories
you've collected so far

509
00:23:17,090 --> 00:23:18,480
and improve the agent over them.

510
00:23:18,480 --> 00:23:28,370

511
00:23:28,370 --> 00:23:33,260
And so DPO is a
spatial technique

512
00:23:33,260 --> 00:23:36,410
based on [INAUDIBLE] where
you can train an LLM using

513
00:23:36,410 --> 00:23:39,230
preference data like
failures and successes,

514
00:23:39,230 --> 00:23:40,980
and use that to improve
the model overall.

515
00:23:40,980 --> 00:23:44,030

516
00:23:44,030 --> 00:23:46,560
And so this is
how agent Q works,

517
00:23:46,560 --> 00:23:48,800
where we create this
Monte Carlo Tree

518
00:23:48,800 --> 00:23:54,620
Search to create trajectories
of successes and failures.

519
00:23:54,620 --> 00:23:56,780
We can then use
self-critic mechanisms

520
00:23:56,780 --> 00:23:59,000
to identify, what are
the proposed actions that

521
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:00,300
actually succeeded and failed?

522
00:24:00,300 --> 00:24:02,330
And then we're able to
pass them through a DPO

523
00:24:02,330 --> 00:24:03,997
to actually go and
optimize the network.

524
00:24:03,997 --> 00:24:08,360

525
00:24:08,360 --> 00:24:10,465
This is an example
of how this works.

526
00:24:10,465 --> 00:24:16,280

527
00:24:16,280 --> 00:24:19,730
So the agent starts
in the first state.

528
00:24:19,730 --> 00:24:21,890
And the task, in
this case, is we

529
00:24:21,890 --> 00:24:28,580
want to go and book a restaurant
reservation on OpenTable.

530
00:24:28,580 --> 00:24:33,050
So first, it makes a mistake
and goes to the home page.

531
00:24:33,050 --> 00:24:35,637
Then it recognizes that it made
a mistake and can backtrack.

532
00:24:35,637 --> 00:24:37,220
So the blue arrow
here shows that it's

533
00:24:37,220 --> 00:24:39,890
going and backtracking.

534
00:24:39,890 --> 00:24:43,898
Then it can go and navigate
to the correct restaurant.

535
00:24:43,898 --> 00:24:46,190
In this case, if the agent
accidentally makes a mistake

536
00:24:46,190 --> 00:24:49,350
and chooses the incorrect date,
then it can again backtrack,

537
00:24:49,350 --> 00:24:50,630
recover back.

538
00:24:50,630 --> 00:24:54,097
Open the date selector,
choose the right date,

539
00:24:54,097 --> 00:24:56,180
open the seat selection,
and then finally complete

540
00:24:56,180 --> 00:24:57,210
the reservation.

541
00:24:57,210 --> 00:25:01,190
And so this is how the
system is learning over time.

542
00:25:01,190 --> 00:25:04,220
It's making a lot of mistakes,
but it's saving the mistakes

543
00:25:04,220 --> 00:25:05,590
and over time improving on them.

544
00:25:05,590 --> 00:25:10,610

545
00:25:10,610 --> 00:25:14,330
We tried Agent Q in a lot
of real world scenarios,

546
00:25:14,330 --> 00:25:17,510
including OpenTable
actual reservations.

547
00:25:17,510 --> 00:25:21,080
So we actually spun up thousands
of robots, more like hundred

548
00:25:21,080 --> 00:25:24,380
thousands of bots
that ran on OpenTable

549
00:25:24,380 --> 00:25:27,830
and used our method to create
agents that are able to book

550
00:25:27,830 --> 00:25:31,340
restaurants, and make
reservations, and do

551
00:25:31,340 --> 00:25:32,460
a bunch of other things.

552
00:25:32,460 --> 00:25:35,600
And we tried this with a lot
of different methods and models

553
00:25:35,600 --> 00:25:36,740
out there.

554
00:25:36,740 --> 00:25:38,870
So we tried GPT 4o
and then we found,

555
00:25:38,870 --> 00:25:40,970
on this OpenTable
reservation task,

556
00:25:40,970 --> 00:25:44,270
we are only able to reach
around 62.6% accuracies.

557
00:25:44,270 --> 00:25:46,220
When it comes to
something like DPO,

558
00:25:46,220 --> 00:25:50,120
the accuracy actually go
to something like 71%.

559
00:25:50,120 --> 00:25:56,870
When we try agent Q, we are able
to make this work much, much

560
00:25:56,870 --> 00:25:57,480
better.

561
00:25:57,480 --> 00:26:01,610
So we are able to reach 81%
accuracy without any MCTS

562
00:26:01,610 --> 00:26:02,820
as part of the method.

563
00:26:02,820 --> 00:26:04,370
And when we apply
the whole technique

564
00:26:04,370 --> 00:26:08,220
with MCTS, and DPO, and the
self-critique mechanisms.

565
00:26:08,220 --> 00:26:11,610
We are actually able to reach
close to 95.4% accuracies.

566
00:26:11,610 --> 00:26:15,290
And this is using a lot of
self-learning for the agent

567
00:26:15,290 --> 00:26:16,550
to improve itself.

568
00:26:16,550 --> 00:26:19,190
This takes usually less
than one day of training

569
00:26:19,190 --> 00:26:26,340
for the agent to go from, I
would say, here, 20% accuracy.

570
00:26:26,340 --> 00:26:28,230
That's 18.6%.

571
00:26:28,230 --> 00:26:31,400
That's roughly 20%,
all the way to 95.4.

572
00:26:31,400 --> 00:26:34,670
So that's a 4x improvement in
agent performance in less than

573
00:26:34,670 --> 00:26:35,170
one day.

574
00:26:35,170 --> 00:26:47,720

575
00:26:47,720 --> 00:26:51,900
As a next topic, I'll touch
on memory and personalization.

576
00:26:51,900 --> 00:26:57,920

577
00:26:57,920 --> 00:27:01,910
So one way to think
about AI agents

578
00:27:01,910 --> 00:27:07,210
is that they are taking
information, processing them.

579
00:27:07,210 --> 00:27:09,730

580
00:27:09,730 --> 00:27:13,610
OK, so imagine you
have an AI model.

581
00:27:13,610 --> 00:27:16,820
What an AI model is doing,
it's taking some prompts.

582
00:27:16,820 --> 00:27:19,240
So it's taking some
language tokens.

583
00:27:19,240 --> 00:27:21,890
And it's outputting some
new language tokens.

584
00:27:21,890 --> 00:27:24,790
And so this is acting
similar to a processor, where

585
00:27:24,790 --> 00:27:26,560
if you have a CPU,
what happens is

586
00:27:26,560 --> 00:27:28,810
you have some instructions,
which are usually binary

587
00:27:28,810 --> 00:27:30,427
encoded that go into the CPU.

588
00:27:30,427 --> 00:27:32,510
And then you have some
instructions that come out,

589
00:27:32,510 --> 00:27:33,830
which are also binary encoded.

590
00:27:33,830 --> 00:27:36,040
And then you do a loop
over them again and again.

591
00:27:36,040 --> 00:27:38,000
And that's how normal
computers work.

592
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:41,560
You can do a very similar
thing and have this abstraction

593
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:47,380
of an AI model as acting similar
to a computer, where you have

594
00:27:47,380 --> 00:27:49,840
language tokens that
are going in that

595
00:27:49,840 --> 00:27:51,100
are encoded in the prompt.

596
00:27:51,100 --> 00:27:53,350
And you have language
tokens coming out.

597
00:27:53,350 --> 00:27:56,530
And this allows you to
think about an AI model

598
00:27:56,530 --> 00:27:59,220
as being a processor that's
operating over natural language.

599
00:27:59,220 --> 00:28:02,463

600
00:28:02,463 --> 00:28:04,630
So this is something that
you can think about GPT 4,

601
00:28:04,630 --> 00:28:06,075
for example, going
and doing this.

602
00:28:06,075 --> 00:28:08,710

603
00:28:08,710 --> 00:28:12,850
This is similar to some of the
older processor like MIPS32 that

604
00:28:12,850 --> 00:28:14,400
used 32-bit instructions.

605
00:28:14,400 --> 00:28:17,290
Right now, if you
look at GPT 4, we

606
00:28:17,290 --> 00:28:20,240
are able to reach very
big context lengths.

607
00:28:20,240 --> 00:28:23,270
So that's very interesting.

608
00:28:23,270 --> 00:28:25,510
And when GPT 4
initially came out,

609
00:28:25,510 --> 00:28:27,110
it was constrained to 8k tokens.

610
00:28:27,110 --> 00:28:29,140
Now, we have 32k
tokens, and 128k tokens,

611
00:28:29,140 --> 00:28:30,838
and 1 million tokens.

612
00:28:30,838 --> 00:28:32,380
So the context length
of these models

613
00:28:32,380 --> 00:28:34,213
is just increasing and
increasing over time.

614
00:28:34,213 --> 00:28:38,620

615
00:28:38,620 --> 00:28:40,210
And as the context
length increases

616
00:28:40,210 --> 00:28:41,610
that also allows us to have--

617
00:28:41,610 --> 00:28:47,260

618
00:28:47,260 --> 00:28:50,410
A question from online can you
speak to the compute budget

619
00:28:50,410 --> 00:28:51,740
for the day long run?

620
00:28:51,740 --> 00:28:55,930
Was it H100s or a cluster?

621
00:28:55,930 --> 00:28:57,040
The results.

622
00:28:57,040 --> 00:28:57,590
Yes.

623
00:28:57,590 --> 00:28:59,120
So that was all H100.

624
00:28:59,120 --> 00:29:03,820
So actually, we trained the
whole models on 50 H100s

625
00:29:03,820 --> 00:29:05,530
in less than one day.

626
00:29:05,530 --> 00:29:06,430
Got you.

627
00:29:06,430 --> 00:29:08,260
And then one
question from before,

628
00:29:08,260 --> 00:29:11,680
as AI agents increasingly
emulate human behavior, what

629
00:29:11,680 --> 00:29:13,750
protocols do you foresee
being implemented

630
00:29:13,750 --> 00:29:16,300
to help users
distinguish between AI

631
00:29:16,300 --> 00:29:19,290
and humans in conversation?

632
00:29:19,290 --> 00:29:20,540
Yeah, that's very interesting.

633
00:29:20,540 --> 00:29:22,900
I think that becomes a
question of security,

634
00:29:22,900 --> 00:29:26,500
of how can we identify whether
it's a human or an agent.

635
00:29:26,500 --> 00:29:28,370
It's actually a very
hard question right now

636
00:29:28,370 --> 00:29:30,162
because you actually
have voice agents that

637
00:29:30,162 --> 00:29:32,110
are effectively
able to mimic humans

638
00:29:32,110 --> 00:29:33,680
and are able to pass as humans.

639
00:29:33,680 --> 00:29:37,090
And that's actually happening
in the real world right now.

640
00:29:37,090 --> 00:29:40,310
Over time, we will need
human proof of identity.

641
00:29:40,310 --> 00:29:42,430
So this could be biometrics.

642
00:29:42,430 --> 00:29:46,180
This could also be a combination
of maybe some personal data,

643
00:29:46,180 --> 00:29:48,760
or some of password, or
secrets that only you know,

644
00:29:48,760 --> 00:29:50,260
and you can use
that to authenticate

645
00:29:50,260 --> 00:29:53,145
that you're talking to an
actual human and not an agent.

646
00:29:53,145 --> 00:30:02,450

647
00:30:02,450 --> 00:30:04,180
Any more questions?

648
00:30:04,180 --> 00:30:05,260
Yeah.

649
00:30:05,260 --> 00:30:15,580
So, [INAUDIBLE] systems fail?

650
00:30:15,580 --> 00:30:18,260
So they did some
comprehensive study.

651
00:30:18,260 --> 00:30:20,680
First of all, they
say MLS has been there

652
00:30:20,680 --> 00:30:22,450
for more than 20 years.

653
00:30:22,450 --> 00:30:25,550
You have distributed systems,
transaction processing.

654
00:30:25,550 --> 00:30:31,270
So we are just having AI
covered by the same name.

655
00:30:31,270 --> 00:30:36,680
And so far, I really
haven't seen anything new,

656
00:30:36,680 --> 00:30:39,310
except for you have
an agent which,

657
00:30:39,310 --> 00:30:41,560
instead of just having
API people coding

658
00:30:41,560 --> 00:30:43,540
all the logic in
the program, you

659
00:30:43,540 --> 00:30:46,210
have an agent will be
able to do something.

660
00:30:46,210 --> 00:30:49,420
If given a prompt, it will
give you some results.

661
00:30:49,420 --> 00:30:53,150
So how can you just putting
all agents together,

662
00:30:53,150 --> 00:30:55,570
the intelligence
suddenly elevated?

663
00:30:55,570 --> 00:30:57,770
So communicating between agents.

664
00:30:57,770 --> 00:31:00,600
My point is, just how
communication between agents

665
00:31:00,600 --> 00:31:03,930
is exactly the same as it
was before 20 years ago.

666
00:31:03,930 --> 00:31:06,420
I think collaboration
between agents

667
00:31:06,420 --> 00:31:09,220
is only vehicle to
elevate intelligence.

668
00:31:09,220 --> 00:31:13,710
But I'm missing that part
today in the lecture.

669
00:31:13,710 --> 00:31:16,620
This is actually something
that's coming next.

670
00:31:16,620 --> 00:31:19,650
But just to answer
your question,

671
00:31:19,650 --> 00:31:21,840
the biggest issue
is just reliability.

672
00:31:21,840 --> 00:31:24,360
What happens is when all
these agents are communicating

673
00:31:24,360 --> 00:31:26,280
using natural
language, that causes

674
00:31:26,280 --> 00:31:30,120
a lot of miscommunication,
where maybe your agent got

675
00:31:30,120 --> 00:31:32,760
the wrong instruction or failed
to understand what's happening.

676
00:31:32,760 --> 00:31:35,370
And the more agents you add,
the more communication overhead

677
00:31:35,370 --> 00:31:36,025
is there.

678
00:31:36,025 --> 00:31:37,650
So you can imagine
if you have an agent

679
00:31:37,650 --> 00:31:40,000
system with n different agents.

680
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:42,670
Then there's n squared
communication rules.

681
00:31:42,670 --> 00:31:44,370
And so the amount of
error in the system

682
00:31:44,370 --> 00:31:47,263
increases as quadratic.

683
00:31:47,263 --> 00:31:49,680
And that makes that allows for
a lot of different mistakes

684
00:31:49,680 --> 00:31:51,060
that can happen.

685
00:31:51,060 --> 00:31:55,080
Suggesting you to
read [INAUDIBLE]

686
00:31:55,080 --> 00:31:57,690
they've all pretty much
all the problems solved,

687
00:31:57,690 --> 00:32:00,790
15 chapters of the book.

688
00:32:00,790 --> 00:32:03,543
Yeah, totally.

689
00:32:03,543 --> 00:32:05,960
That could be very interesting
also for the audience here.

690
00:32:05,960 --> 00:32:09,060

691
00:32:09,060 --> 00:32:14,070
So let's come back to this.

692
00:32:14,070 --> 00:32:18,600
So one way to think
about agents is when you

693
00:32:18,600 --> 00:32:19,990
have this transformer model.

694
00:32:19,990 --> 00:32:22,750
The transformer model is
acting as a processor.

695
00:32:22,750 --> 00:32:24,430
So it's taking
this input prompts.

696
00:32:24,430 --> 00:32:26,790
And it's giving out
the output prompts.

697
00:32:26,790 --> 00:32:28,980
And what you want
to do is you want

698
00:32:28,980 --> 00:32:30,820
to be able to have
a memory system.

699
00:32:30,820 --> 00:32:33,240
So you want to have something
like a file disk or RAM,

700
00:32:33,240 --> 00:32:38,048
where you are saving
what's happening and being

701
00:32:38,048 --> 00:32:39,340
able to process that over time.

702
00:32:39,340 --> 00:32:41,007
So you want to have
repeated operations.

703
00:32:41,007 --> 00:32:42,760
So you do the first
pass over a model.

704
00:32:42,760 --> 00:32:44,050
You get some output tokens.

705
00:32:44,050 --> 00:32:46,380
You can save them in
a RAM-like system.

706
00:32:46,380 --> 00:32:49,090
And then you have some new
instructions that come out.

707
00:32:49,090 --> 00:32:51,370
Like now, here's
step two of the plan.

708
00:32:51,370 --> 00:32:52,060
Go execute that.

709
00:32:52,060 --> 00:32:52,870
Here's step three of the plan.

710
00:32:52,870 --> 00:32:54,078
Here's step four of the plan.

711
00:32:54,078 --> 00:32:57,550
And that looping behavior,
this is, in a sense,

712
00:32:57,550 --> 00:33:01,950
giving rise to agents, where
you can imagine this is--

713
00:33:01,950 --> 00:33:04,120
the transformer
is the processor.

714
00:33:04,120 --> 00:33:07,140
The memory system, and the
instructions, and the planning

715
00:33:07,140 --> 00:33:10,780
are acting similar to the
file system and the RAM.

716
00:33:10,780 --> 00:33:14,340
And so there are
overall giving rise

717
00:33:14,340 --> 00:33:16,470
to this computer
architecture, where

718
00:33:16,470 --> 00:33:21,450
you have the agent acting
as a computer system

719
00:33:21,450 --> 00:33:26,560
with the memory processors,
which is the compute

720
00:33:26,560 --> 00:33:29,730
and then being able to
use browsers, and actions,

721
00:33:29,730 --> 00:33:34,170
and multi-modality, which can
be inputs like audio, and voice,

722
00:33:34,170 --> 00:33:34,700
and so on.

723
00:33:34,700 --> 00:33:39,210

724
00:33:39,210 --> 00:33:43,230
When we think about
long-term memory,

725
00:33:43,230 --> 00:33:46,110
based on the analogy
before, you can

726
00:33:46,110 --> 00:33:49,680
think of this as similar to
a disk, where you want a user

727
00:33:49,680 --> 00:33:52,980
memory that's long
lived and persistent.

728
00:33:52,980 --> 00:33:55,600
And so that you can save
context about the user.

729
00:33:55,600 --> 00:33:59,130
You can load that on the
fly whenever you want.

730
00:33:59,130 --> 00:34:02,460
There's different mechanisms
for long-term memories.

731
00:34:02,460 --> 00:34:04,900
The prevalent one is embeddings.

732
00:34:04,900 --> 00:34:06,630
So you have
retrieval models that

733
00:34:06,630 --> 00:34:09,840
can go and fetch the right
user embeddings on the fly.

734
00:34:09,840 --> 00:34:14,909
So if I have a question like,
OK, does this person, Joe,

735
00:34:14,909 --> 00:34:17,070
is he allergic to peanuts?

736
00:34:17,070 --> 00:34:19,000
Then can the system
go and find that?

737
00:34:19,000 --> 00:34:21,489
And if we have a lot of
user data about the user,

738
00:34:21,489 --> 00:34:26,717
then we can use a retrieval
model to do an embedding lookup.

739
00:34:26,717 --> 00:34:28,800
Find out, OK, if this is
something that we already

740
00:34:28,800 --> 00:34:30,010
know about the user or not.

741
00:34:30,010 --> 00:34:34,290
And based on that,
make a right judgment.

742
00:34:34,290 --> 00:34:37,208
And this is something
that is very important.

743
00:34:37,208 --> 00:34:39,875
You are able to see early traces
of this in systems like ChatGPT

744
00:34:39,875 --> 00:34:42,060
right now.

745
00:34:42,060 --> 00:34:43,710
There's still a lot
of open questions

746
00:34:43,710 --> 00:34:45,690
when it comes to
long-term memory.

747
00:34:45,690 --> 00:34:47,730
The first one is hierarchy.

748
00:34:47,730 --> 00:34:52,940
How do we decompose memory into
more graphical structures, where

749
00:34:52,940 --> 00:34:56,130
you can have
temporal persistence.

750
00:34:56,130 --> 00:34:57,690
You can have more structures.

751
00:34:57,690 --> 00:35:00,107
And you might also want to
think about memory as something

752
00:35:00,107 --> 00:35:03,300
that is adaptable because human
memory is usually not static.

753
00:35:03,300 --> 00:35:04,350
It's changing over time.

754
00:35:04,350 --> 00:35:05,808
And so you also
want to think about

755
00:35:05,808 --> 00:35:07,890
when you have agent
memory, how can it change?

756
00:35:07,890 --> 00:35:09,000
How can it be dynamic?

757
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:10,410
How can it self-adjust?

758
00:35:10,410 --> 00:35:12,300
Because the systems
are also learning.

759
00:35:12,300 --> 00:35:13,170
They're improving.

760
00:35:13,170 --> 00:35:15,850
And what does this dynamic
memory systems look like?

761
00:35:15,850 --> 00:35:25,460

762
00:35:25,460 --> 00:35:30,770
And memory leads
to personalization,

763
00:35:30,770 --> 00:35:33,920
where the goal with
having long-term memory

764
00:35:33,920 --> 00:35:38,270
is that you can personalize
this agents to the user.

765
00:35:38,270 --> 00:35:40,230
And they're able to
understand what you like,

766
00:35:40,230 --> 00:35:41,870
what you don't like.

767
00:35:41,870 --> 00:35:44,540
And they're aligned
with your preferences.

768
00:35:44,540 --> 00:35:47,210
So if you have this
case of maybe someone

769
00:35:47,210 --> 00:35:49,100
is allergic to
peanuts and you want

770
00:35:49,100 --> 00:35:52,150
to have an agent that's
ordering food on Door Dash,

771
00:35:52,150 --> 00:35:53,800
then you want it
to be personalized,

772
00:35:53,800 --> 00:35:55,550
so it doesn't accidentally
order something

773
00:35:55,550 --> 00:35:58,080
that you're allergic to, and
how can you go and build that.

774
00:35:58,080 --> 00:36:00,460
And everyone has
different preferences,

775
00:36:00,460 --> 00:36:01,300
likes, and dislikes.

776
00:36:01,300 --> 00:36:02,460
So when you're
designing agents, it's

777
00:36:02,460 --> 00:36:04,170
very important to
actually make sure

778
00:36:04,170 --> 00:36:05,765
that you can account for this.

779
00:36:05,765 --> 00:36:07,890
So there might be a lot of
explicit personalization

780
00:36:07,890 --> 00:36:10,320
information that
you can collect.

781
00:36:10,320 --> 00:36:11,350
What does the user like?

782
00:36:11,350 --> 00:36:12,642
Are they allergic to something?

783
00:36:12,642 --> 00:36:14,160
What are their favorite dishes?

784
00:36:14,160 --> 00:36:19,150
What seat preferences they have,
if they're flying, and so on.

785
00:36:19,150 --> 00:36:21,250
There's also a lot of
implicit preferences.

786
00:36:21,250 --> 00:36:24,070
So there's a lot of things
around, which brand do you like?

787
00:36:24,070 --> 00:36:26,610
Do you like Adidas versus Nike?

788
00:36:26,610 --> 00:36:28,560
If there were 10
items on a list,

789
00:36:28,560 --> 00:36:32,100
suppose you're looking for a
housing, which one do you prefer

790
00:36:32,100 --> 00:36:32,740
and why?

791
00:36:32,740 --> 00:36:34,200
And those things
are very implicit.

792
00:36:34,200 --> 00:36:36,100
So they're not explicitly known.

793
00:36:36,100 --> 00:36:37,985
And then there are
mechanisms where

794
00:36:37,985 --> 00:36:40,110
you can collect a lot of
these implicit preferences

795
00:36:40,110 --> 00:36:41,402
and then personalize over time.

796
00:36:41,402 --> 00:36:45,730

797
00:36:45,730 --> 00:36:47,730
There's a lot of challenges
when you're building

798
00:36:47,730 --> 00:36:49,580
these personalization systems.

799
00:36:49,580 --> 00:36:52,140
The first one is just
user privacy and trust.

800
00:36:52,140 --> 00:36:55,580
How do you actually go
and actively collect

801
00:36:55,580 --> 00:36:56,640
this information?

802
00:36:56,640 --> 00:37:00,620
And how do you get people
to give that to you?

803
00:37:00,620 --> 00:37:02,330
There's different
methods you can

804
00:37:02,330 --> 00:37:04,680
go and use to actually
collect this information.

805
00:37:04,680 --> 00:37:08,150
So one is just active learning,
where you're explicitly asking

806
00:37:08,150 --> 00:37:09,660
the user for their preferences.

807
00:37:09,660 --> 00:37:11,930
You're asking them, OK, are
you allergic to something?

808
00:37:11,930 --> 00:37:13,847
Or do you have this seat
preference and so on?

809
00:37:13,847 --> 00:37:15,630
And there might also
be passive learning,

810
00:37:15,630 --> 00:37:17,865
where if you can
record the users

811
00:37:17,865 --> 00:37:19,490
and see what they're
doing, then you're

812
00:37:19,490 --> 00:37:21,448
able to passively learn
from their preferences.

813
00:37:21,448 --> 00:37:25,228
Maybe this person likes Nike
shoes because that's where--

814
00:37:25,228 --> 00:37:27,020
what you have seen them
do on the computer.

815
00:37:27,020 --> 00:37:28,853
And the agent is learning
from your behavior

816
00:37:28,853 --> 00:37:31,790
and become better and better.

817
00:37:31,790 --> 00:37:35,222
And you can learn to personalize
by supervised fine tuning, where

818
00:37:35,222 --> 00:37:36,930
you are collecting a
lot of interactions.

819
00:37:36,930 --> 00:37:38,597
This can also be
through human feedback,

820
00:37:38,597 --> 00:37:40,440
where you can get
thumbs up or thumbs down

821
00:37:40,440 --> 00:37:42,470
and use that to improve--

822
00:37:42,470 --> 00:37:45,482
like this agent go and
do the right thing.

823
00:37:45,482 --> 00:37:47,190
And this is something
similar to ChatGPT,

824
00:37:47,190 --> 00:37:49,803
where if you like the
chat outputs, then

825
00:37:49,803 --> 00:37:50,970
you can give it a thumbs up.

826
00:37:50,970 --> 00:37:52,380
If you don't like it, you
give it a thumbs down.

827
00:37:52,380 --> 00:37:54,365
And then this can be
used to personalize

828
00:37:54,365 --> 00:37:55,240
the system over time.

829
00:37:55,240 --> 00:37:58,430

830
00:37:58,430 --> 00:38:01,890
So now going to agent
to agent communication.

831
00:38:01,890 --> 00:38:05,090

832
00:38:05,090 --> 00:38:06,090
One question online.

833
00:38:06,090 --> 00:38:08,930
How do you do evaluations
on the performance of agents

834
00:38:08,930 --> 00:38:10,320
that collaborate with humans?

835
00:38:10,320 --> 00:38:12,020
And is it a moving target?

836
00:38:12,020 --> 00:38:15,620
At what point is human
performance redundant and agents

837
00:38:15,620 --> 00:38:17,540
can be fully autonomous?

838
00:38:17,540 --> 00:38:19,050
I would say it's
a hard question.

839
00:38:19,050 --> 00:38:21,178
You just have to go
and build benchmarks

840
00:38:21,178 --> 00:38:22,970
because it's very hard
to know what's going

841
00:38:22,970 --> 00:38:24,740
to happen in the real world.

842
00:38:24,740 --> 00:38:28,850
Right now, I would say, based
on a lot of current state

843
00:38:28,850 --> 00:38:31,880
of evaluations and
what I showed before,

844
00:38:31,880 --> 00:38:33,050
agents are not really there.

845
00:38:33,050 --> 00:38:34,790
The most successful
agents we have seen so far

846
00:38:34,790 --> 00:38:35,700
are coding agents.

847
00:38:35,700 --> 00:38:39,335
So if you have your, whatever,
intelligent code editor.

848
00:38:39,335 --> 00:38:42,200

849
00:38:42,200 --> 00:38:43,790
You can already see the traces.

850
00:38:43,790 --> 00:38:45,790
They are automating a lot
of engineering for you

851
00:38:45,790 --> 00:38:47,560
already, that you don't
have to go and write

852
00:38:47,560 --> 00:38:48,935
a lot of boilerplate
code, or you

853
00:38:48,935 --> 00:38:51,220
have to spend a lot of
your own time fixing bugs.

854
00:38:51,220 --> 00:38:53,890
So at some point, we'll see this
thing where humans become more

855
00:38:53,890 --> 00:38:55,927
like managers.

856
00:38:55,927 --> 00:38:57,260
And we are giving them feedback.

857
00:38:57,260 --> 00:38:59,980
We are giving them
direction, OK, we want this--

858
00:38:59,980 --> 00:39:01,888
suppose you have systems
of different agents.

859
00:39:01,888 --> 00:39:04,180
So you give them, OK, I want
agent 1 to go and do this.

860
00:39:04,180 --> 00:39:06,250
Agent 2 to go and
do this, so on.

861
00:39:06,250 --> 00:39:07,610
See what the final output is.

862
00:39:07,610 --> 00:39:12,760
And over using that, improve
the overall generation process

863
00:39:12,760 --> 00:39:14,470
that you are going towards.

864
00:39:14,470 --> 00:39:16,600
And so this is likely
what's going to happen

865
00:39:16,600 --> 00:39:19,600
is the agent systems will become
better and better executors,

866
00:39:19,600 --> 00:39:21,460
where the humans
become the managers

867
00:39:21,460 --> 00:39:22,920
for the systems of agents.

868
00:39:22,920 --> 00:39:29,980

869
00:39:29,980 --> 00:39:33,580
So when it comes to agent
to agent communication,

870
00:39:33,580 --> 00:39:38,590
we think about
multi-agent architectures

871
00:39:38,590 --> 00:39:40,030
and multi-agent
systems, where you

872
00:39:40,030 --> 00:39:43,048
have all this cute little
digital robots that

873
00:39:43,048 --> 00:39:44,840
can go and talk to each
other, communicate,

874
00:39:44,840 --> 00:39:48,192
and go do your work
in a very coordinated

875
00:39:48,192 --> 00:39:49,150
and streamlined manner.

876
00:39:49,150 --> 00:39:51,830

877
00:39:51,830 --> 00:39:53,960
There's reasons that
you want to go and build

878
00:39:53,960 --> 00:39:55,400
multi-agent systems.

879
00:39:55,400 --> 00:39:58,880
The first one is
parallelization.

880
00:39:58,880 --> 00:40:01,340
By dividing a task
into smaller chunks

881
00:40:01,340 --> 00:40:03,660
and having multiple agents.

882
00:40:03,660 --> 00:40:05,520
If you have n agents
instead of one agent,

883
00:40:05,520 --> 00:40:10,160
you can improve the overall
speeds and efficiencies.

884
00:40:10,160 --> 00:40:12,740
The second thing
is specialization.

885
00:40:12,740 --> 00:40:14,817
If you have different
specialized agents--

886
00:40:14,817 --> 00:40:16,400
so you have maybe a
spreadsheet agent,

887
00:40:16,400 --> 00:40:18,990
and you have a Slack agent, and
you have a web browser agent.

888
00:40:18,990 --> 00:40:20,210
Then you can also
route different tasks

889
00:40:20,210 --> 00:40:21,300
to different agents.

890
00:40:21,300 --> 00:40:23,790
Each agent can become really,
really good at their tasks.

891
00:40:23,790 --> 00:40:27,980
And this is similar to having
a degree in a specific major

892
00:40:27,980 --> 00:40:31,110
or having an occupation--
specializing in that occupation.

893
00:40:31,110 --> 00:40:36,862

894
00:40:36,862 --> 00:40:39,320
There's a lot of challenges
when it comes to agent to agent

895
00:40:39,320 --> 00:40:41,150
in communication.

896
00:40:41,150 --> 00:40:45,800
The biggest one is that
this communication is lossy.

897
00:40:45,800 --> 00:40:49,530
When you have one agent company
communicating to another agent,

898
00:40:49,530 --> 00:40:53,270
it's possible that it
might make a mistake.

899
00:40:53,270 --> 00:40:55,650
This is similar to what
happens in human organizations.

900
00:40:55,650 --> 00:40:57,330
Maybe your manager will
ask you to go do something,

901
00:40:57,330 --> 00:40:59,830
but you maybe misunderstood
them and did something different

902
00:40:59,830 --> 00:41:01,680
or they were like, oh,
why does this happen?

903
00:41:01,680 --> 00:41:04,730
And similarly, agent
to agent communication

904
00:41:04,730 --> 00:41:07,640
is also fundamentally
lossy, where

905
00:41:07,640 --> 00:41:09,390
whenever you are
communicating information

906
00:41:09,390 --> 00:41:11,765
from one agent to another,
you are losing some percentage

907
00:41:11,765 --> 00:41:12,600
of the information.

908
00:41:12,600 --> 00:41:15,380
And that allows for mistakes
to propagate in that system

909
00:41:15,380 --> 00:41:18,480
and become like
increasingly more prevalent.

910
00:41:18,480 --> 00:41:22,520

911
00:41:22,520 --> 00:41:25,518
And there's different mechanisms
for multi-agent system.

912
00:41:25,518 --> 00:41:27,060
This is a very novel
field right now.

913
00:41:27,060 --> 00:41:28,860
People are still trying
to figure this out.

914
00:41:28,860 --> 00:41:31,460
No one has actually
cracked this right now.

915
00:41:31,460 --> 00:41:33,950
What you want to do
is you want to build

916
00:41:33,950 --> 00:41:35,690
the right system of
hierarchies, where

917
00:41:35,690 --> 00:41:38,030
you might have
manager agents that

918
00:41:38,030 --> 00:41:39,470
are working with worker agents.

919
00:41:39,470 --> 00:41:41,220
You might have managers
or manager agents.

920
00:41:41,220 --> 00:41:44,170
And you might have maybe
flat agents organizations,

921
00:41:44,170 --> 00:41:46,730
where maybe one manager is
managing hundreds of agents,

922
00:41:46,730 --> 00:41:50,050
or it could be a
vertical tree, where

923
00:41:50,050 --> 00:41:53,832
you have maybe 10 different
hierarchies of agents

924
00:41:53,832 --> 00:41:55,040
that are managing each other.

925
00:41:55,040 --> 00:41:57,170
And so a lot of these
systems are possible.

926
00:41:57,170 --> 00:41:59,170
And this just depends on
the task of what you're

927
00:41:59,170 --> 00:42:02,020
doing and specializing on.

928
00:42:02,020 --> 00:42:04,778
And the biggest challenge
with this kind of systems

929
00:42:04,778 --> 00:42:06,820
is, how do you exchange
communication effectively

930
00:42:06,820 --> 00:42:08,960
without losing that information?

931
00:42:08,960 --> 00:42:10,460
How do you build
syncing primitives?

932
00:42:10,460 --> 00:42:13,150
How can communication
from one agent

933
00:42:13,150 --> 00:42:16,570
that's very far away from maybe
another agent in the hierarchy

934
00:42:16,570 --> 00:42:19,572
go and be communicated
very, very effectively

935
00:42:19,572 --> 00:42:20,280
across the chain?

936
00:42:20,280 --> 00:42:25,240

937
00:42:25,240 --> 00:42:27,520
There's a couple of
frameworks out there

938
00:42:27,520 --> 00:42:30,010
that are looking to solve
these problems on how do we

939
00:42:30,010 --> 00:42:32,300
make this communication
protocols robust

940
00:42:32,300 --> 00:42:38,140
and how can we add mechanisms
to reduce this miscommunication.

941
00:42:38,140 --> 00:42:41,870
A big one in this part is MCP,
which is model context protocol.

942
00:42:41,870 --> 00:42:44,500
This is a protocol that
came from Anthropic

943
00:42:44,500 --> 00:42:46,520
that a lot of people
are using right now.

944
00:42:46,520 --> 00:42:48,350
It's a simple
wrapper around APIs.

945
00:42:48,350 --> 00:42:52,210
So what it does is it gives you
a streamlined standard format

946
00:42:52,210 --> 00:42:54,190
around each API.

947
00:42:54,190 --> 00:42:58,640
And by creating an MCP
wrapper around your service--

948
00:42:58,640 --> 00:43:01,120
so this could maybe you
have a file server service

949
00:43:01,120 --> 00:43:02,540
that exposes an API.

950
00:43:02,540 --> 00:43:04,790
You can create MCP wrapper
for your file server,

951
00:43:04,790 --> 00:43:07,450
or maybe for your email client,
or for maybe your Slack client,

952
00:43:07,450 --> 00:43:09,530
or something running
on your computer.

953
00:43:09,530 --> 00:43:11,770
Then all this MCP
connected servers

954
00:43:11,770 --> 00:43:15,680
can go and communicate with each
other and do things for you.

955
00:43:15,680 --> 00:43:17,980
And so this allows for very
effective communication,

956
00:43:17,980 --> 00:43:19,750
where you are able to
control the routing

957
00:43:19,750 --> 00:43:22,125
and make the systems modular.

958
00:43:22,125 --> 00:43:24,375
So you're able to plug-in
new services as you want to.

959
00:43:24,375 --> 00:43:28,690

960
00:43:28,690 --> 00:43:31,090
Similarly, another
framework in this space

961
00:43:31,090 --> 00:43:32,390
is Agent to Agent protocol.

962
00:43:32,390 --> 00:43:36,483
So this is a new protocol that
came from Google very recently,

963
00:43:36,483 --> 00:43:38,400
that's allowing for
agents to also communicate

964
00:43:38,400 --> 00:43:40,858
with other agents and add a
lot of reliability and fallback

965
00:43:40,858 --> 00:43:41,680
mechanisms.

966
00:43:41,680 --> 00:43:48,780

967
00:43:48,780 --> 00:43:51,575
I'm not sure how many people
here in the room have used MCPs.

968
00:43:51,575 --> 00:43:54,690

969
00:43:54,690 --> 00:43:55,510
Yeah, not many.

970
00:43:55,510 --> 00:43:58,230
OK, cool.

971
00:43:58,230 --> 00:44:00,400
So MCPs are actually very cool.

972
00:44:00,400 --> 00:44:04,680
What they are doing is they
are abstracting your APIs

973
00:44:04,680 --> 00:44:07,740
and making them
very, very modular

974
00:44:07,740 --> 00:44:13,990
so that you can go and plug
your API into an MCP protocol.

975
00:44:13,990 --> 00:44:15,700
And once it's
wrapped around that,

976
00:44:15,700 --> 00:44:17,640
then you can go
and interconnect it

977
00:44:17,640 --> 00:44:21,070
to any other service
that's supported by MCPs.

978
00:44:21,070 --> 00:44:26,940
So it becomes, in a sense, like
having a standard interface

979
00:44:26,940 --> 00:44:31,320
for communication for
your different services

980
00:44:31,320 --> 00:44:33,918
or applications you have, and
exposing them, and letting them

981
00:44:33,918 --> 00:44:35,210
connect and talk to each other.

982
00:44:35,210 --> 00:44:36,730
So similar to how
you have something

983
00:44:36,730 --> 00:44:40,760
like HTTPS for communication
on the normal internet,

984
00:44:40,760 --> 00:44:43,150
MCP becomes an interesting
protocol for communication

985
00:44:43,150 --> 00:44:45,195
to happen over different agents.

986
00:44:45,195 --> 00:44:49,420

987
00:44:49,420 --> 00:44:54,490
And so if you have a client
like Claude, or Replit,

988
00:44:54,490 --> 00:44:58,240
or some other model,
you can connect that

989
00:44:58,240 --> 00:45:02,000
to servers that are
supporting the MCP protocol.

990
00:45:02,000 --> 00:45:04,040
You can have a bunch
of different services.

991
00:45:04,040 --> 00:45:07,947
Each services could be some of
data tool, like a database, API,

992
00:45:07,947 --> 00:45:08,780
or pretty much else.

993
00:45:08,780 --> 00:45:11,280
And they can all interconnect
and do modular things for you.

994
00:45:11,280 --> 00:45:14,590

995
00:45:14,590 --> 00:45:18,280
And because MCPs are not
dependent on the spec

996
00:45:18,280 --> 00:45:19,870
of your API.

997
00:45:19,870 --> 00:45:24,100
They can allow you to absorb a
lot of changes and at this level

998
00:45:24,100 --> 00:45:26,080
of modularity and
abstraction by standardizing

999
00:45:26,080 --> 00:45:27,200
the whole interface.

1000
00:45:27,200 --> 00:45:29,770

1001
00:45:29,770 --> 00:45:31,780
You can also have
dynamic tool discovery

1002
00:45:31,780 --> 00:45:35,490
because you can find
different MCP servers that

1003
00:45:35,490 --> 00:45:37,412
are exposed in some directory.

1004
00:45:37,412 --> 00:45:39,120
And then you can also
plug in MCP servers

1005
00:45:39,120 --> 00:45:42,840
that you like and
connect to them.

1006
00:45:42,840 --> 00:45:46,260
So you can plug in new
tools, spread them out.

1007
00:45:46,260 --> 00:45:49,265
And you can route information
based on what you want to do.

1008
00:45:49,265 --> 00:45:54,300

1009
00:45:54,300 --> 00:45:57,750
Finally, touching on some
of the issues when it

1010
00:45:57,750 --> 00:46:00,060
comes to agent systems.

1011
00:46:00,060 --> 00:46:03,720
So far, we have seen a lot
of different things, OK,

1012
00:46:03,720 --> 00:46:04,998
how can this agents work?

1013
00:46:04,998 --> 00:46:06,040
How can we evaluate them?

1014
00:46:06,040 --> 00:46:07,270
How can we train them?

1015
00:46:07,270 --> 00:46:08,910
How can we think
about communicating

1016
00:46:08,910 --> 00:46:12,300
with different agent systems?

1017
